# The Windows package toolchain — a third manifest, and it earns its keep.
#
# `requirements.in` is the product (and what the Raspberry Pi installs);
# `requirements-dev.in` is the workbench (and what CI installs to run the
# gates). Neither is the right home for what follows:
#
# - pystray and pillow are RUNTIME dependencies, but only of the frozen Windows
#   app. Putting them in requirements.in would build pillow on every Pi for a
#   tray icon that machine has no desktop to draw.
# - pyinstaller is BUILD tooling, but only for the release. Putting it in
#   requirements-dev.in would make every CI test run install it to do nothing.
#
# Install it only when building a package:
#   .venv\Scripts\python -m pip install -r requirements-package.txt

-r requirements.in

# System-tray icon for the packaged app: it owns the process lifecycle, so
# there is no terminal window to keep open and no terminal window to close by
# accident. pillow is pystray's image backend, used to load the icon.
pystray>=0.19
pillow>=10.0

# One-folder bundle. Not one-file: one-file unpacks to a temp directory on every
# launch, which is slow for a ~40 MB app and, more importantly, is the exact
# behaviour that makes an on-disk database dangerous to resolve against the code.
pyinstaller>=6.10
